{"event": "result", "result": {"conversation_id": "13ec9614-020b-42a5-8ea0-2f1c9b61c230", "status": "SUCCESS", "response": "I have inspected the `admission.py`, `cli.py`, `ranking.py`, and `capacity.py` files to determine the fallback behavior when the primary ZAI route is rejected due to hostwide capacity reaching 26. \n\nHere are the key findings:\n1. **Fallback Candidates**: Out of the supported `ADAPTER_ROUTES` (`grok`, `antigravity`, `zai`), when `zai` hits its concurrency ceiling (26), it is marked invalid by `check_provider_capacity`. Furthermore, `grok` is also unconditionally rejected when checking capacity due to a freeze policy. This leaves **`antigravity`** as the single fallback candidate.\n2. **Seed Determinism**: The `plan` command explicitly uses a deterministic `seed=0` to evaluate candidates cleanly as a dry-run projection. The `run` command uses `seed=int(time.time() * 1000)` which is non-deterministic across runs but is completely reproducible if known since it is recorded directly into the `provenance` payload for auditability.\n3. **Capacity Filter**: The `check_capacity=True` argument drops capacity-limited models from `valid_routes` in `validate_quse`, placing them into `rejections`. As a result, they never reach `select_candidate` to be ranked.\n\nI have compiled these findings and written them to the specified report: [capacity-ranking-report.json](file:///home/alexey/git/cloudflare-agent-git/.local/scratch/fleet_work/capacity-ranking-report.json).\n", "duration_seconds": 141.325986555, "num_turns": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 124630, "output_tokens": 6822, "thinking_tokens": 5482, "cache_read_tokens": 280777, "total_tokens": 131452}}}
